WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Kubernetes scheduling and runtime behavior depend heavily on resource requests and limits.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.480
A request tells the scheduler how much CPU or memory the workload expects to need.

3
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.420
A limit defines the boundary the container is not supposed to exceed.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.200
These settings influence node placement, capacity planning, noisy-neighbor behavior, and failure modes.

5
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:34.980
For Node.js services, they should be based on measured behavior instead of copied from another manifest because traffic shape, dependency latency, request size, and application design all change resource demand.

6
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.260
Memory requires particular care.

7
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:45.920
A Node.js process has its own heap behavior, native memory use, buffers, dependencies, and garbage collection patterns.

8
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.540
If the container memory limit is too low, the process may be killed when memory exceeds the allowed boundary.

9
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.960
In Kubernetes this often appears as OOMKilled or repeated restarts.

10
00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.140
If memory requests are too high, the cluster may waste capacity.

11
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:06.080
If limits are absent, one workload may consume more shared capacity than intended.

12
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.980
The right answer usually comes from load testing, telemetry, and iteration.

13
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.340
CPU controls also affect user experience.

14
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:28.560
CPU limits can change latency and throughput under load, especially for request-heavy services, expensive JSON processing, encryption, compression, or synchronous work that blocks the event loop.

15
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.340
Requests influence scheduling and autoscaling inputs.

16
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:39.480
A service may look fine in a small test but behave badly when a replica is throttled during peak traffic.

17
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:49.740
Observing CPU, request latency, event-loop symptoms, queueing, and dependency delays together is more useful than treating CPU as an isolated number.

18
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.180
Resource controls are reliability and security controls.

19
00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:00.520
They help prevent one workload from consuming shared cluster capacity and degrading unrelated services.

20
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:05.740
They also force teams to understand the expected operating envelope of the service.

21
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:19.600
A practical workflow is to start with reasonable defaults, test with representative traffic, review production telemetry, adjust requests and limits, and revisit those settings when code, traffic, or dependencies change.

22
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.780
Kubernetes gives you the controls, but measurement gives those controls meaning.
